  "notes": "The architect of the EDM-pop vocal-feature model, across four documented eras (French house to 2008, electro-house pop crossover 2009-2012, big room 2013-2018, future rave 2019-present); the modelled centre is the crossover and big-room band the entry gives, 125-130. Two board facts drive the kit. The electro-house grammar is \"a big four-square kick, huge layered clap on 2&4, 16th hats, and long riser/white-noise builds\", so the clap offset is wide, the accent tier is at the ceiling and the roll frequency is high. And the big-room era \"strips percussion back to 'only a kick or tom and a couple of hi-hats' for maximum drop contrast\" — which is why the perc lanes are riser/impact/tom at 0-2 hits a bar, the sparsest hand-percussion setting in this section. Harmony is \"deliberately archetypal so any guest topline will sit on it\" (i-VI-III-VII is named as the workhorse), so the families are few and triad-heavy and the melody is written as a guest topline rather than a riff. The bass is \"distorted sawtooth sidechained hard to the kick\". ⚠ The research marks the harmony `[LOW-CONF]` and calls the drum and harmonic specifics electro-house/big-room convention; both marks are repeated here, and every velocity, density and probability above is INFERRED.",
